namespace AvCharts.Demo;

/// <summary>The glyphs the catalogue's rows wear, one per page.</summary>
/// <remarks>
/// <para>
/// Lucide's own 24-unit stroke set - the same geometry strings, character for character, that
/// Lucide.Avalonia hands to its geometry parser. They are written down here rather than read from that
/// package because the package cannot run in a browser at all: it decompresses its icon set with
/// <c>System.IO.Compression.BrotliStream</c>, and the WebAssembly runtime ships that whole type as a stub
/// that throws - every member of it does, the constructor included (measured against 10.0.12 of the
/// browser runtime pack). A gallery that has to run in a browser therefore cannot ask that library for an
/// icon, whatever the browser.
/// </para>
/// <para>
/// Data rather than geometry, deliberately: the catalogue is read while the tests are being discovered, on
/// a thread that has no platform behind it, and a geometry built there starts a render loop on that thread
/// - which every test that draws then fails to use. What the shell does with a path is
/// <c>Geometry.Parse</c> it while it builds the row, by which time there is a platform and a thread that
/// owns it.
/// </para>
/// <para>
/// Lucide is ISC licensed, and its shapes are drawn - not filled - in a 24-unit box, so what a row needs is
/// a <c>Glyph</c> with the path in its <c>Data</c> and a stroke: see <c>iPaas.Controls.Glyph</c>, which is
/// how the house's own chrome draws the same set.
/// </para>
/// </remarks>
